---
import BaseLayout from '~/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '~/components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '~/components/Footer.astro';

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  'datePublished': '2026-07-28',
  'dateModified': '2026-07-29',
  '@graph': [
    {
      '@type': 'Article',
      'headline': 'Chengdu 3-Day Itinerary: The Perfect First Visit',
      'description': 'A carefully paced three-day plan covering pandas, food, ancient streets, and hidden corners most tourists miss.',
      'url': 'https://best-of-chengdu.com/blog/chengdu-3-day-itinerary',
      'author': {'@type': 'Organization', 'name': 'Best of Chengdu'},
    },
    {
      '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
      'itemListElement': [
        {'@type': 'ListItem', 'position': 1, 'name': 'Home', 'item': 'https://best-of-chengdu.com/'},
        {'@type': 'ListItem', 'position': 2, 'name': 'Blog', 'item': 'https://best-of-chengdu.com/blog'},
        {'@type': 'ListItem', 'position': 3, 'name': '3-Day Itinerary', 'item': 'https://best-of-chengdu.com/blog/chengdu-3-day-itinerary'},
      ],
    },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout schemaJson={JSON.stringify(schema)} title="Chengdu 3-Day Itinerary" description="A carefully paced three-day plan covering pandas, food, ancient streets, and hidden corners most tourists miss.">
  <Header />

  <main>
    <article class="max-w-[860px] mx-auto px-6 pt-20 pb-24">
      <span class="text-xs px-2 py-0.5 rounded-full bg-accent/10 text-accent font-medium">Itineraries</span>
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-display font-semibold tracking-tighter leading-[1.1] mt-4">
        Chengdu 3-Day Itinerary: The Perfect First Visit
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">Three days is enough to fall in love with Chengdu. This itinerary gives you the pandas, the food, the ancient streets, and a few surprises most first-time visitors miss.</p>

      <section class="mt-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-display font-semibold tracking-tight mb-4">Day 1: Pandas & Ancient Streets</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
          <p><strong class="text-[#1a1a1a]">Morning (7:30am-12pm): Chengdu Panda Base.</strong> Arrive by 8am. Pandas are most active in the morning, especially in cooler months. Follow our <a href="/attractions/panda-base" class="text-accent hover:underline">Panda Base guide</a> for the best route. The nursery and sub-adult enclosures are the highlights.</p>
          <p><strong class="text-[#1a1a1a]">Lunch (12:30pm):</strong> Take the shuttle back to the city and head to Chen Mapo Tofu near Wenshu Monastery. Order the namesake dish, twice-cooked pork, and a vegetable. Budget 60-80 RMB per person.</p>
          <p><strong class="text-[#1a1a1a]">Afternoon (2-5pm): Kuanzhai Alley.</strong> Three parallel Qing-dynasty alleys with courtyard tea houses, artisan shops, and incredible people-watching. Start in Wide Alley for the grand courtyards, then explore Narrow Alley for a more intimate feel.</p>
          <p><strong class="text-[#1a1a1a]">Evening (6pm): Jinli Ancient Street.</strong> Arrive at dusk when the red lanterns come on. The snack street is touristy but the atmosphere is unbeatable. Grab a few skewers and walk the length of the street. End with a drink at a riverside bar near Jiuyanqiao.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="mt-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-display font-semibold tracking-tight mb-4">Day 2: Food Deep Dive</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
          <p><strong class="text-[#1a1a1a]">Morning (9am): People's Park.</strong> Start your day like a local. Grab a bamboo chair at <a href="/food/tea-houses" class="text-accent hover:underline">Heming Tea House</a>, order jasmine tea (15 RMB), and watch the world go by. If you're feeling social, the matchmaking corner in the north of the park is pure Chengdu theater.</p>
          <p><strong class="text-[#1a1a1a]">Lunch (12pm): Street food crawl on Jianshe Road.</strong> This is the city's best street food strip. Start with dan dan noodles (12 RMB), follow with a guo kui stuffed flatbread (8 RMB), and finish with skewers. Don't fill up too much.</p>
          <p><strong class="text-[#1a1a1a]">Afternoon (2-4pm): Wuhou Shrine.</strong> A serene temple complex dedicated to Zhuge Liang. The ancient cypress trees and red-walled corridors are beautiful even if you know nothing about Three Kingdoms history. The neighboring Jinli connects directly if you want to revisit it in daylight.</p>
          <p><strong class="text-[#1a1a1a]">Dinner (7pm): Hotpot.</strong> This is the main event. Book a table at <a href="/food/hotpot" class="text-accent hover:underline">Shu Daxia or Xiaolongkan</a>. Order the nine-grid pot, beef slices, tripe, tofu skin, and lotus root. Dip everything in sesame oil with garlic and chopped scallions. Budget 80-120 RMB per person.</p>
          <p><strong class="text-[#1a1a1a]">Late night (10pm):</strong> Walk off the hotpot along the Jinjiang River. Stop for a craft beer at a Yulin neighborhood bar if you still have room.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="mt-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-display font-semibold tracking-tight mb-4">Day 3: Culture & Hidden Corners</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
          <p><strong class="text-[#1a1a1a]">Morning (9am): Wenshu Monastery.</strong> A working Buddhist temple with the best vegetarian restaurant in the city (try the imitation meat dishes, they're surprisingly good). The surrounding lanes are full of antique shops and old tea houses missed by most tourists.</p>
          <p><strong class="text-[#1a1a1a]">Midday: Sichuan Opera at Shufeng Yayun.</strong> Book a matinee if available, or an early evening show. The face-changing (bian lian) is the highlight. <a href="/experiences/sichuan-opera" class="text-accent hover:underline">Read our full guide</a> for ticket tips and best seats.</p>
          <p><strong class="text-[#1a1a1a]">Afternoon: Taikoo Li & Daci Temple.</strong> The contrast between the ancient temple and the surrounding luxury mall is pure Chengdu. Walk through the temple courtyard, then browse Taikoo Li's open-air design. The IFS panda climbing the building is a required photo.</p>
          <p><strong class="text-[#1a1a1a]">Farewell dinner: Yu's Kitchen or Ming Ting.</strong> For a splurge, book Yu's Kitchen (500+ RMB, reservations required weeks ahead). For something more casual, Ming Ting is consistently excellent at 60-100 RMB. Either way, end with bingfen (ice jelly) for dessert.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="mt-12 p-6 rounded-xl bg-surface">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-display font-semibold tracking-tight mb-4">Practical tips</h2>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 text-sm text-gray-500">
          <div>
            <p class="font-semibold text-[#1a1a1a] mb-1">Getting around</p>
            <p>The metro covers almost everything on this itinerary. Get a transportation card or use Alipay's QR code. Didi for late-night rides (15-30 RMB per trip).</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="font-semibold text-[#1a1a1a] mb-1">Budget</p>
            <p>Mid-range: 500-700 RMB/day (comfortable hotel, restaurants, attractions). Budget: 200-300 RMB/day (hostel, street food, metro).</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="font-semibold text-[#1a1a1a] mb-1">Best months</p>
            <p>March-May and September-November. <a href="/travel/best-time-to-visit" class="text-accent hover:underline">Read the seasonal guide</a> for details.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="font-semibold text-[#1a1a1a] mb-1">Book ahead</p>
            <p>Panda Base tickets, hotpot reservations, and Yu's Kitchen should be booked in advance. Use Ctrip or ask your hotel concierge.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </article>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>